'' A DEFINITION OF AN EXTERN IS EMITTED UNDER THE NAME THE DECLARATION LINKS BY --
'' 'E$', not the module spelling 'e' -- so the two halves of one object meet. See the
'' source for fbc's four measured symbols; tests/mir/extern-definition-links is the
'' same fact at the linker, across two modules.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/extern-definition-name.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 E$ = 5
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 SE$ = 6
'' An ALIAS names the link symbol, and the DEFINITION is what resolves against it.
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 zz = 7
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 ce = 8
'' The folded spellings are what the storage used to be called. None may survive: a
'' 'global var i32 e' beside 'E$' would be the bug, two objects where fbc has one.
'' EXPECT-NOT: global var i32 e =
'' EXPECT-NOT: global var i32 se =
'' EXPECT-NOT: global var i32 a =
